Text:
This document specifies the syntax of a design document, and is itself a design document.
Design documents are a crucial tool to document behaviour independently of implementations.
We can see a program as three layers:
The implementation of a program is one layer.
The tests (in test driven development style) are a more abstract layer.
But this layer is connected with the implementation: to compile the tests we need the structure of the implementation, in particular the user relevant defined types.
A design is more abstract: it has stories instead of tests, grammar instead of user defined types, and well formedness instead of representation invariants.
It also contains required directions of state transitions, pseudocode and, very occasionally, English text like this one.
Implementation notes bridge the gap to the implementation, or explain how an abstract step is possible.
A grammar is crucial for each shape that two programs share, and sometimes needed for an internal state.
The literature on user stories and on test driven development often overlooks grammar: in some domains code is mostly methods of existing datatypes, so the grammar comes for free.

Grammar:
DesignScope ::= Document1 .. Documentn //by going 1..n it can be empty with n=0
Document ::= Section1 "\n" .. "\n" Sectionn //sections separated by new line
Section ::= TextSection               \
          | GrammarSection            \
          | WellFormednessSection     \
          | StorySection              \
          | DefineSection             \
          | NotationSection           \
          | InvariantSection          \
          | ImplementationNotesSection //we can use \ to escape the new line
TextSection ::= "Text:", " "*, "\n", TextLines
TextLines   ::= Line1..Linen //we will often use 's' to mean many.
Line        ::= AnyText, "\n" //two components: AnyText followed by a new line
AnyText     ::= any sequence of Unicode characters that do not represent a new line.
GrammarSection ::= "Grammar:", " "*, "\n", GrammarLine1 .. GrammarLinen
GrammarLine ::= Nonterminal, " "*, "::=", Line //we can use * and + as in regexes
WellFormednessSection ::= "Well formedness:", " "*, "\n", WellFormednessLines
WellFormednessLines   ::= WellFormednessLine1 .. WellFormednessLinen
StorySection ::= "Story:", " "*, "\n", StoryLines
DefineSection ::= "Define ", AnyText, ":", " "*, "\n", StoryLines
NotationSection ::= Notation0 .. Notationn
Notation ::= "Notation: ", Line | "Notation: ", AnyText, " where:", "\n", WhereLine0 .. WhereLinen
WhereLine ::= "- ", Line
InvariantSection ::= "Invariant:", " "*, "\n", Line1 .. Linen
ImplementationNotesSection ::= "Implementation notes:", " "*, "\n", Line1 .. Linen
WellFormednessLine ::= "in", " "+, Nonterminal, ":", Line
StoryLines ::= StoryLine1 .. StoryLinen
StoryLine ::= Label, " ", Keyword, " ", Line
Label ::= [A-Z]
Keyword ::= "event" | "action" | "after" | "fact" | "if"
Nonterminal ::= [A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*

Well formedness:
in DesignScope: nonTerminalsOf(Document1) disjoint .. disjoint nonTerminalsOf(Documentn)
in Document: only Sectionn can be of form ImplementationNotesSection.
in Document: if Sectioni is of form WellFormednessSection, Section(i-1) is of form GrammarSection\
   and nonTerminalsOf(Section(i-1)).containsAll(wellFormedHeadsOf(Sectioni))
//Note how we can use .containsAll and any other 'reasonable but made up' notation
in StoryLines: the Label of StoryLinei is the i-th letter from A to Z.
in StoryLine: when the Keyword is "if", the Line is AnyText, " return ", Line or AnyText, " error ", Line.
in Notation: the first line of Notation ends with " where:" only when Notation has a WhereLine.

Notation: nonTerminalsOf(Section1 "\n" .. "\n" Sectionn) = nonTerminalsOf(Section1),..,nonTerminalsOf(Sectionn)

Notation: nonTerminalsOf("Grammar:", " "*, "\n", GrammarLine1 .. GrammarLinen) = nonTerminalOf(GrammarLine1),..,nonTerminalOf(GrammarLinen)

Notation: nonTerminalsOf(Section) = empty list where:
- Section not of form GrammarSection

Notation: wellFormedHeadsOf("Well formedness:", " "*, "\n", WellFormednessLine1 .. WellFormednessLinen) = wellFormedHeadOf(WellFormednessLine1),..,wellFormedHeadOf(WellFormednessLinen)

Notation: wellFormedHeadOf("in", " "+, Nonterminal, ":", Line) = Nonterminal
//This is kind of verbose; notation WellFormednessLine.Nonterminal can be used here.
//In general A.B extracts the leftmost non terminal in the grammar tree of A that is of form B

Text:
The GrammarLines and the WellFormednessLines of all of the Documents of a DesignScope form one unified grammar.
The 'right side' of a GrammarLine is the text after "::=".
The line below
Pizza ::= something cooked in the oven 
is a GrammarLine.
A precise right side is a sequence of components separated by ", ", or alternatives separated by " | ".
A precise component is a Nonterminal, a string literal holding the text to match,
or a sequence X0..Xn (non empty) or X1..Xn (can be empty).
The double quotes of a string literal can be left out when the text is clear without them: Infos.list(Project1 .. Projectn) stands for "Infos.list(", Project1 .. Projectn, ")".
A text matching the right side of N, but breaking a WellFormednessLine of N, is not an N.
For example, with grammar
A ::= "aa" K "bb"
K ::= "c" | "d"
and well formedness line
in A: A.K is not of form "d"
aacbb is of form A.
aadbb is not of form A.

Text:
Grammars and Notations are a compact and modern mathematical metalanguage to define inductive definitions.
Example on the lambda calculus, showing how the notation has changed across time.
In the oldest form, a set is the smallest set closed under some conditions, and a function is defined by cases.
The terms of the lambda calculus form the smallest set E such that:
  x in E, for each x in the set V of the variables;
  if x in V and e in E, then λx.e in E;
  if e in E and e' in E, then e e' in E.
The substitution e[x:=e'] is defined by cases:
  x[x:=e'] = e'
  y[x:=e'] = y, if y != x
  (e1 e2)[x:=e'] = e1[x:=e'] e2[x:=e']
  (λx.e)[x:=e'] = λx.e
  (λy.e)[x:=e'] = λy.(e[x:=e']), if y != x and y is not free in e'
In this old set up the substitution was actually a function, not a way to add elements to the set _ = _.
The reduction is the smallest relation R on E such that:
  ((λx.e) e', e[x:=e']) in R;
  if (e1, e1') in R, then (e1 e2, e1' e2) in R;
  if (e2, e2') in R, then (e1 e2, e1 e2') in R.
Pierce, Types and Programming Languages, section 3.2, writes the conditions defining a set as inference rules.
The premises of a rule are above the line, and the conclusion of the rule is below the line.
  x in V      x in V   e in E      e in E   e' in E
  ------      ---------------      ----------------
  x in E         λx.e in E            e e' in E
The name of the set is still written in every premise and in every conclusion.
For a relation, Pierce writes e -> e' instead of (e, e') in R:
  ---------------------      e1 -> e1'
  (λx.e) e' -> e[x:=e']      ---------------
                             e1 e2 -> e1' e2
In section 3.5, Pierce gets an instance of a rule by replacing each metavariable, such as e or e1, with the same term in the conclusion and in all the premises.
Substitution stays defined by cases, as in Pierce, section 5.3.
More recently:
A 'metarule' is a rule with metavariables, written without the name of the set that the rule defines.
A group of metarules defines one set or one relation, and the group is annotated with the name of that set or relation, as in [e].
Inside the group [e], the symbol e stands for both the set and an element of the set.
The premise x in V disappears, since x is the metavariable for the variables.
  [e]
  ---     e          e   e'
  x       ----       ------
          λx.e       e e'
The grammar e ::= x | λx.e | e e is now a shortcut for this group of metarules.
Well formedness on the grammar is just side conditions on the metarules. This is why well formedness does restrict the application of the metarules that the grammar represents.
A case of a function defined by cases is a metarule too, and the condition of the case is a premise:
  [e[x:=e'] = e'']
                    y != x            y != x   y notIn FV(e')  e''= e[x:=e']
  ------------      ------------      ---------------------------------------
  x[x:=e] = e       y[x:=e] = y       (λy.e)[x:=e'] = λy.(e'')
Note how in this stage we needed to write e''= e[x:=e'] and λy.(e''); 
being able to write (λy.e)[x:=e'] = λy.(e[x:=e']) is a further relaxation: given that the syntax is fixed
e[x:=e'] = e'', we know that the thing after the = must be an e, and no e is of form λy.(e[x:=e']); thus we can interpret relations over `=` as replaceable, and assume that e[x:=e'] must stand for the application of an `=` relation over `e`. This can be sometimes confusing, but it does save a large amount of space and improves readability by avoiding many metavariables.
Example with ..
A lambda with many parameters needs "..", and ".." comes from metarules too.
A metarule with ".." stands for one metarule for each n.
Choosing n writes x1 .. xn as the n metavariables x1, x2, .., xn, with the text on both sides of ".." between two of them. Expansion also works for constants, for example A1 ",".."," An stands for A1 "," A2 "," A3 with n=3
Then replacing each metavariable with a value gives a rule, as before.
The grammar e ::= x | λx1,..,xn.e | e(e1,..,en)
With well formedness
- in λx1,..,xn.e: x1..xn disjoint 
is a shortcut for
  [e]
  ---     e  x1..xn disjoint      e0 .. en
   x       -----------          ------------
          λx1,..,xn.e            e0(e1,..,en)
  [e -> e']//not complete, does not matter, example formalism
  ----------------------------------------------      e -> e'
  (λx1,..,xn.e)(e1,..,en) -> e[x1:=e1]..[xn:=en]      ---------------------------
                                                      e(e1,..,en) -> e'(e1,..,en)
The first metarule uses one n for the parameters and for the arguments, so the first metarule applies only when there are as many arguments as parameters.
Choosing n=2 in the first metarule gives the metarule
  [e -> e']
  -------------------------------------
  (λx1,x2.e)(e1,e2) -> e[x1:=e1][x2:=e2]
and replacing x1, x2, e, e1 and e2 with a, b, a, c and d gives the rule
  [e -> e']
  -----------------------------
  (λa,b.a)(c,d) -> a[a:=c][b:=d]
Note how we could use .. on the application of the operator [x:=e]
More recently, notations are used instead of actual metarules to save space, since they are in 1-1 relation anyway. Lambda calculus with multiple arguments and call by value may look like this in the modern mathematical metalanguage:
e::= x | v | e(es)
v::= λxs.e
well formedness
in λx1,..,xn.e: x1..xn are disjoint
where xs, es and vs are often implicitly defined as
xs::=x1,..,xn   es::=e1,..,en   vs::=v1,..,vn
xs and es are real metavariables and can have indexes and primes etc; es1..esn is a sequence of sequences.
Define e1->e2:
v(v1,..,vn) -> e[x1=v1]..[xn=vn]
  where: v = λx1,..,xn.e
e(es) -> e'(es)
  where: e->e'
v(vs e es) -> v(vs e' es)
  where: e->e'
Define e[x=v] =e'
x[x=v] = v
x[x'=v] = x
  where: x != x'
e0(e1,..,en)[x=v] = e0[x=v](e1[x=v],..,en[x=v])
(λx1,..,xn.e) [x=v] = (λx1,..,xn.e[x=v])
  where: x notin x1..xn
As you can see in this last case we had to add () to disambiguate; in papers usually we would use different fonts to not mix the () of the grammar to the () of the metalanguage.
The design documents use this last form of metalanguage, where all is either in grammar or in notation form, and add definitions and stories to include side effects.

Text:
A grammar is not an algebraic data type.
A value of an algebraic data type is made by exactly one constructor, and that constructor fixes the type of the value.
A text is 'of form N' when the text matches the right side of N and respects the WellFormednessLines of N.
One text can be of form many Nonterminals.
A grammar is not a parser either: a parser needs a grammar where each text has at most one parse tree, and a design does not.
For example, e ::= N | e "+" e is a good grammar for a design.
The text 1+2+3+4 is of form e in many ways, such as (1+2)+(3+4) and 1+(2+(3+4)), and a design never talks about one of those ways.
Parsing in computer science, as in parsing algorithms and parser generators, is very far from this use of grammars.
A parser finds the one parse tree of a text, and a grammar for a parser is shaped to avoid ambiguity.
Here a grammar defines sets of texts, one way to be of form N is enough, and ambiguity is normal and often useful.
A note for the agent reading this document:
- you are not trained on this mathematical metalanguage;
- you are trained on deceivingly similar topics: parsers, parsing algorithms, abstract syntax trees, algebraic data types;
- the instincts from those topics feel right here and are often wrong, for example reading A.Bs as the children of one parse tree, or reading a Nonterminal in a StoryLine as the set of all its values;
- after writing or changing a design text, always do a separate pass that checks, with reasoning and not with instinct, that each line relies on the semantics of this metalanguage instead of your training: a Nonterminal is a metavariable and is instantiated, "of form" refers to the set, there are no parse trees, and the meaning never depends on how a grammar groups.
Formally, with
  Symbol ::= every character that can be used
a text is a sequence of Symbols, and a 'subsequence' of a text is a contiguous part of that text.
A text is of form e when there is a way to iteratively replace subsequences with Nonterminals until only the Nonterminal e is left.
A subsequence can be replaced with a Nonterminal N when the subsequence matches the right side of N and respects the WellFormednessLines of N.
There are no parse trees and no nodes: there are texts, and the Nonterminals each text is of form.
This is not parsing a text into a tree, as these examples show:
- with Word ::= [a-z]+ and Pair ::= Word "o" Word, the text foobar is of form Word, and also of form Pair as f o obar and as fo o bar: a text has no tokens, and a Word can be cut anywhere;
- with e ::= N | e "+" e, (1+2+3+4).es contains 1+2, 2+3 and 3+4, while no tree for 1+2+3+4 has both 1+2 and 2+3 as nodes;
- with Name ::= [a-z]+ and Let ::= "let " Name, (let x).Names contains let, et, t and x: the characters of a string literal of a grammar are Symbols like any other.
"of form Foo" and "not of form Foo" are a special syntax where Foo is not a metavariable but refers to the set of all Foo. There, Foo is not replaced by a value.
For example, in "e[x=v] is of form v" the two v do not clash: the second v is not a metavariable.
The relation e[x=v]=e' relates e[x=v] to an e', not to a v; "e[x=v] is of form v" holds when that e' is also of form v.
For example, Map1 Map2 is of form Map would check if the two maps, when composed, do produce a map (that is, respect the well formedness of Map)
A.B is the leftmost element of form B in the grammar tree of A; A.Bs is the list of all the elements of form B in the grammar tree of A, from left to right.
When many elements of form B start at the leftmost position, A.B is the longest of them.
There is no tree: the elements of the grammar tree of A are all the subsequences of A.
Reading a text as every Nonterminal the text is of form makes many definitions short:
- every v is an e, with no constructor turning a v into an e;
- with xs ::= x1 .. xn, the text x1 .. xn xs is an xs': joining two lists needs no definition;
- in v(vs e es) above, the arguments of a call are split into the values vs, one argument e and the arguments es, with no index to find the argument e;
- in reachable(_ Node1 "(" _ Node _ ")" _, Node1) below, "_" finds Node1 and Node anywhere in the Graph.
Reading a text as every Nonterminal the text is of form also needs care.
For example, with grammar
  Digit ::= [0-9]
  N ::= Digit | N Digit
  B ::= "0" | "1" | B "0" | B "1"
  Num ::= N | B
101001 is of form B and of form N, so 101001 is a Num in two ways.
101001.N is 101001.
101001.Ns is not just 101001: every subsequence of 101001 is of form N, so 101001.Ns contains also 1, 0, 10, 01 and 0100.
When needed, make the syntax less ambiguous:
  NN ::= Digit | NN Digit
  BB ::= "0" | "1" | BB "0" | BB "1"
  N ::= "base10(" NN ")"
  B ::= "base2(" BB ")"
  Num ::= N | B
Now base2(101001) is of form B and not of form N, and base2(101001).Ns is empty.
The second grammar behaves more predictably than the first grammar, but less flexibly.
As another example, DesignScope.GrammarLines contains also the line "Pizza ::= something cooked in the oven" of a TextSection above, since that Line is of form GrammarLine.
This is why nonTerminalsOf looks only inside the sections of form GrammarSection.

Text:
A Notation adds to a relation: the relation is the smallest relation closed under all the Notations of that relation.
Many Notations add to the relation "=", as the Notations of e[x=v]=e' above.
One may worry about making sure a single value is returned so that something is still a function.
This is a misunderstanding. With foo,bar1,bar2 and beer of form x; consider
foo[bar1=λbeer.beer]=foo. This element is present in e[x=v]=e'
foo[bar2=λbeer.beer]=foo is also present in e[x=v]=e'. By symmetry and transitivity, we can conclude that
foo[bar1=λbeer.beer]=foo[bar2=λbeer.beer]
This makes perfect sense, but also shows that _[_=_] is not a function. It never was.
It is a piece of syntax, that includes the terminals [=] and connects syntaxes that are 'logically' equal to each other but in different syntactical forms.
Note how, technically speaking, foo[bar1=λbeer.beer] is not of form e; since the terminals [=] are not in any e.
A term of a relation nested in a bigger term, as e[x=v] in λy.(e[x=v]), is a meta-notation, like "..".
The metarule has a new metavariable e'' in place of e[x=v], and the premise e[x=v]=e''.
The relation _=_ has no rule replacing equals with equals inside a bigger term: such a rule would relate λy.(e) to λy.(e[x=v]), and λy.(e[x=v]) is not of form e.
A Notation can add to any other relation: "in", "<", "<=", or a relation made for the design, such as e1->e2 or G|-e:T.
For example, a graph is written as its nodes, each node followed by the list of its successors:
  Graph ::= Node1 "(" Nodes1 ")" .. Noden "(" Nodesn ")"
  Nodes ::= Node1 " " .. " " Noden
Three Notations add to the relation "in" the nodes reachable from a node:
  Notation: Node in reachable(Graph, Node)
  Notation: Node in reachable(_ Node1 "(" _ Node _ ")" _, Node1)
  Notation: Node2 in reachable(Graph, Node0) where:
  - Node1 in reachable(Graph, Node0)
  - Node2 in reachable(Graph, Node1)
In a form, "_" stands for any text that keeps the form valid; two "_" can stand for two different texts.
Node not in reachable(Graph, Node0) is implicitly defined as "Node in reachable(Graph, Node0)" not present in the inductively defined relation _ "in" _.

Text:
A Notation reads only its parameters.
Data that a program holds in memory and changes over time, such as a map, reaches a Notation as a parameter: a Define reads that data and passes that data to the Notation.
A Define holds for a call when each fact of that Define holds when the call reaches that fact.
When a fact does not hold, that Define is not the way the call goes, in the same way that a Story whose fact does not hold is not the story that happens; another Define of the same method can hold instead.

Text:
A 'string literal' is the text between two double quotes of one line, with the escapes of the string literals of Java.
A 'comment' starts with a "//" outside every string literal, and ends at the "\n" of its line.
A 'full line comment' has only spaces before it on its line; any other comment is an 'end of line comment'.
Full line comments can be anywhere and are ignored when parsing design documents.
End of line comments, with the new line ending them, behave like "\n" when parsing design documents.
A "\\" that is the last character of a line joins that line with the next line, before string literals and comments are found.
Outside comments, a "\\" followed by any number of spaces and an end of line comment, or by one or more spaces and "\n", is an error.

Story:
A action The agent writes new design documents Document1..Documentn.
B action The agent calls DesignDocumentsParser$check(Document1..Documentn).
C after The check fails.
D action The agent fixes the design document syntax, producing Document'1..Document'n.
E action The agent amends the open PR with Document'1..Document'n.

Text:
A StoryLine has a Keyword after its Label.
With fact, the StoryLine states a condition.
With event, the StoryLine states something happening outside of the program, such as a message arriving.
With action, the StoryLine states a step that the program or the user takes, such as a call of a method.
With after, the StoryLine states a condition caused by the actions before.
With if, the StoryLine is "if c return X" or "if c error X", for a condition c: when c holds, the StoryLine ends the call, or the story, with the result X or with the error X; when c does not hold, the next StoryLine follows.
For example, "fact m(X)" states that the call m(X) has started, and the StoryLines after it show what m(X) does; "action m(X)" makes the call m(X).
The StoryLines of a section happen, or hold, in the order of their Labels.
A condition holds from its StoryLine until a later StoryLine states a contradicting condition, or states that the condition does not hold any more.
Each DefineSection is one way a call of the method it defines can go.
Define is a section to be used when there are steps in time, and side effects.
Notation is a section to be used where there are no steps in time.
A method doing something that later facts can observe, that is a method with side effects, has "$" before its name: DesignDocumentsParser$check.
A method doing only math rewriting on data in memory, with no disk access, has "." before its name: .containsAll.
A Notation 'holds' for a call when every WhereLine of that Notation holds for that call.
A call of a method with "$" is 'undefined' when no DefineSection of that method holds for that call.
A term with a method with ".", such as ProjectsInfo.edit(Message), is 'undefined' when the inductively defined relation of that method relates that term to no element of the expected set: ProjectsInfo.edit(Message) is undefined when no ProjectsInfo' makes ProjectsInfo.edit(Message)=ProjectsInfo' an element of the relation _=_.
The condition "No other Define of m(X) holds" holds when no other DefineSection of m holds for m(X).
The action "return X" ends the call, with the result X; when X is a call with no result, "return X" makes the call X and ends the call with no result.
The action "error X" ends the call, with the error X.
In a StoryLine or a Notation, a Nonterminal stands for one value of that Nonterminal, not for the set of its values.
For example, "the folder holds ProjectName.fearless" is about one ProjectName, and needs no other name for that value.
Every occurrence of a Nonterminal in one Notation, or in one section that is not a NotationSection, stands for the same value; numbered Nonterminals, as in TypeName1 .. TypeNamen, stand for several values.
That is:  TypeName TypeName1 TypeName' TypeName'1 TypeName''2 are five different TypeName
The content of the specification is instantiated on the non terminals and is supposed to be read over them in the same way a metarule is instantiated by replacing the metavariables with the concrete values.
Consider 
  BinNum::= 0 | 1 | 0 BinNum | 1 BinNum
and the line in a Story/Definition
  A fact BinNum 01001110 is even.
This is wrong: BinNum is a metavariable, thus it is instantiated in the line, so the concrete line would read, for example, as the following nonsense:
  A fact 011101 01001110 is even.
The set of all stories/defines is supposed to be understood not as a full set of behaviour, but as a set of tests that a reasonable implementation would be able to use to infer the full behaviour.
For example if we have a story saying
  A fact The bread costs 40
  B fact The butter costs 20
  C action We pay 60
We expect that the implementation can infer that we aim to sum the costs for an arbitrary amount of items. No need to give infinite stories.
  A fact The name "Foo" is taken
  B fact The name "Foo2" is free
  C action We adopt "Foo2"
Here, the implementation can infer that if "Foo2" is also taken, we may try "Foo3"
An action that changes a value uses "=", "+=" or "-=": m= {} empties the map m, m+= (Key->Value) makes m map Key to Value, and m-= (Key->Value) removes (Key->Value) from m.
A condition on a map uses "in" or "not in": (Key->Value) in m, and (Key->Value) not in m; "_" stands for any value.
(Key1->Value1) .. (Keyn->Valuen) is a map when Key1..Keyn are disjoint
map1+=map2 instead would override the keys in map1 with the ones in map2.
map1 map2 is the concatenation of the two maps, thus it is a map only if all the keys are disjoint.
Text:
A good design document starts with a few Text lines, followed by a GrammarSection and a WellFormednessSection.
In a good design, wisdom is used to decide if a call in a StoryLine, such as DesignDocumentsParser$check(Document1..Documentn), needs a DefineSection or not. Eventually we need to delegate to intuitive names or elements implemented in other software not covered by a design document.

Implementation notes:
To call DesignDocumentsParser$check(Document1..Documentn)
Use the Java script designs/parser/DesignDocumentsParser.java of ZeroToHero, with Java 25 or later.
The script takes one folder: Document1..Documentn are the files directly in that folder.
Example command line, from the folder of ZeroToHero:
java designs/parser/DesignDocumentsParser.java designs
The check prints nothing when it succeeds; otherwise the check prints each error on standard error as file:line: text, and ends with exit code 1.
